# How this clone is really built

## What to write here

Everything true of {{ company_name }}'s operator that `manifest.toml` cannot
express, and that therefore exists only because somebody wrote it down.
`docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` stamps the declared configuration — addresses, adapters,
scopes, knobs — and then points here for the rest. This file is the rest.

Write a short titled paragraph per subject. The ones that usually matter:

- **Provenance.** Where this clone came from, what was removed when it was
  re-based onto the shared kernel, and which files are template-owned rather
  than yours.
- **What was authored in `company/customer-service-playbook.md` and
  `company/mailbox-identity.md`.** The voice, the signature, the
  recipient-language rule, the product mechanics — those files ARE the
  record, so this is the place to say how they got their current shape and
  whether `cs instructions --commit` matches what is in them right now.
- **The send boundary as it actually stands.** Which paths can reach a
  customer, what denies them today, and what a human still has to do.
- **Mechanisms with a history.** A check that exists because something went
  wrong once, and would look arbitrary without that sentence.
- **Anything surprising about accounts, Drive scope, or per-project memory** —
  the places where the obvious reading of a verb is not what it does.
- **Known gaps.** What is unfinished, unfilled or unverified right now, and
  what nobody should assume works.

Date a claim whose truth could expire, and say what you actually ran to check
it.

## Why it matters

This is the only durable place for it. `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` and `AGENTS.md`
are rendered from the kernel template and are replaced wholesale on every
`cs update` — anything written into them is content living in a file the next
release overwrites. Files under `company/` are the opposite: the kernel creates
one only if it is missing and never touches it again, so what you write here
survives every upgrade. That difference is exactly why this file exists.

An agent opening this repo reads the stamped configuration and believes it.
What it cannot infer is why the configuration is that shape — and that is the
part a new session, or a colleague, or you in six months, will actually need.
